    <text type="letter">
        <body>
            <div type="writingSession">
                <pb n="1"/>
                <opener>
                    <dateline><date when="1947-03-10">March 10, 1947</date></dateline><salute>Dear
                            <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__4927" type="person">Mr.
                        Davidson</rs>:</salute>
                </opener>
                <p>As I understand <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__752" type="person">Dr. Hannah
                        Hafkesbrink</rs> of Connecticut College, <rs ref="#tillich_place_id__835"
                        type="place">New London</rs>, <rs ref="#tillich_place_id__726" type="place"
                        >Conn.</rs> has submitted to you a manuscript in which you might be
                    interested for publication.<note type="ea">Es handelt sich hierbei um das
                        Manuskript zu: <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__752" type="person">Hanna
                            Hafkesbrink</rs>: <rs type="work" ref="#tillich__D2I2Q9TH">Unknown
                            Germany: An Inner Chronicle of the First World War based on Letters and
                            Diaries</rs> (1948).</note> I think it would be very important that <rs
                        type="work" ref="#tillich__D2I2Q9TH">Dr. Hafkesbrink&apos;s book</rs> be
                    published. I read it very carefully and was deeply impressed by the material
                    used and the interpretation given. The book illuminates a period of the history
                    of German culture and thought which is decisive for the understanding of the
                    present situation and which inspite of this is very little known even to the
                    scholars of history.</p>
                <p>The selection of the letters and the other material is excellent and very
                    representative; the style is warm and vivid but the author never oversteps the
                    limit of scientific objectivity. Not only everybody who deals vocationally with
                    the history of German culture, but a larger public interested in the state of
                    mind of the German people in the period of the world wars would be potential
                    readers of the book.</p>
                <closer>
                    <salute>Sincerely yours,</salute>
                    <signed><rs ref="#tillich_person_id__1928" type="person">Paul
                        Tillich</rs><lb/>Professor at Union Theological Seminary, <rs
                            ref="#tillich_place_id__319" type="place">New York City</rs></signed>
                </closer>

            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
